course: everyday
lesson: 340
title: Copying and peeking in tests
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Cheating ends in different ways, but it never ends well. Ella in Auckland peeked at her neighbour's spelling test because she hadn't learned the words. Nobody saw. She got ten out of ten — and the mark felt empty, because it wasn't really hers. Her conscience bit until she told the teacher and took the test again at lunch.
explain: Max in Dublin copied his friend's maths homework twice and got away with it, so he thought he always could. The third time they both had the same wrong answer. The teacher noticed, his friend got in trouble too, and Max's planner came home to be signed. He said it was his fault, not his friend's, and did the sheet again alone.
explain: When Max let his mum build his project for the school competition and said he did it, the teacher asked him how he made the tower, and he didn't know. The tower could not be entered, because it wasn't his work. His parents were called in to talk with the teacher; at home there was a row and the console went away for a week. Max slammed his door and said «It's not fair!» and didn't speak to Mum all evening.
explain: By the morning the sulk had faded. He said sorry, and Mum explained that the lost console was a consequence, not revenge. He made a smaller, wobblier tower by himself — and was prouder of it than of the perfect one.
fact: A mark you get by cheating does not show what you know, so it feels empty.
fact: Copying homework hurts the friend who helped you, because he can get in trouble too.
fact: Getting away with cheating a few times does not make it safe; it is often noticed in the end, for example by the same mistakes.
fact: The answers in the back of a book teach you nothing; doing the working does. A teacher can spot copied answers: every answer is right, but there is no working.
fact: A test with cheating on it usually doesn't count, and a note may go home.
fact: Pretending to be fouled in sport is cheating with your body and can earn a yellow card; saying sorry to the other team's player helps put it right.
fact: Cutting a corner in a race is cheating too: a runner seen doing it is taken off the results.
fact: Your own imperfect work is worth more than someone else's perfect work with your name on it.
fact: A consequence like losing a console for a week is meant to teach, not to take revenge.
fact: Feeling cross and sulking after a consequence is normal, but putting things right is still your job.
fact: Owning up and asking for help with the part you didn't understand is the real fix for cheating in learning.
exercise: Why did Ella peek at her neighbour's spelling test? | She hadn't learned the words and was scared | She wanted to check her neighbour's spelling | The teacher said they could share | She couldn't see the board
exercise: Nobody saw Ella peek. Why did her ten out of ten feel empty? | She knew the mark wasn't really hers | Her friend got a gold star as well as ten | Ten is not a good mark | The teacher didn't smile
exercise: What did Ella do to put it right? | Told the teacher and took the test again | Peeked less the next time, only at hard words | Gave her mark to her neighbour | Said nothing and forgot it
exercise: Max copied homework twice without being caught. What did that make him believe? | That he could always get away with it | That copying is how you learn the fastest | That his teacher didn't care about it at all | That his friend really wanted him to copy it
exercise: How was Max's copying found out the third time? | They both had the same wrong answer | His friend told on him at once to the teacher | He wrote his friend's name on it | A camera saw him
exercise: Why is copying unfair to the friend who let you? | He can get in trouble too | He loses his homework book | He has to do it all over twice | It isn't unfair to him at all
exercise: A girl copies answers from the back of the book. How does the teacher notice? | Every answer is right, but there is no working | The answers are written in a different colour of pen | The book has a lock on it | The teacher reads her mind
exercise: What is the real fix after copying answers from the back? | Own up and ask the teacher to explain what she couldn't do | Rub out a few answers so that the page looks normal and nobody can tell | Copy more carefully next time | Say the book fell open
exercise: A boy writes the spellings on his hand and the ink rubs off on the paper. What usually happens? | The test doesn't count and a note goes home | He gets a prize for good spelling and neat work | Nothing, ink on paper is normal | The teacher gives everyone extra marks
exercise: A boy falls over in football before anyone touches him, to win a penalty. What is this? | Cheating with his body, and it can earn a yellow card | A clever and fair trick | Good acting that the rules allow | An accident nobody can blame
exercise: Max's mum built his project. Why couldn't he enter the competition? | It wasn't his work, and he couldn't explain it | The tower was too tall to fit through the door | His mum was not in his class | He forgot to bring it
exercise: Max lost his console for a week. What was it, according to Mum? | A consequence to teach him, not revenge | A punishment because she was angry with him | A way to keep the console for herself to play | A mistake she would undo the very next day
exercise: Max slammed the door and said «It's not fair!» What is true about his sulking? | The feeling is normal, but putting it right is still his job | Sulking for long enough always makes Mum give in and hand it back | Sulking proves the punishment was wrong | Slamming doors is a good way to say sorry
exercise: A boy cuts a corner in the cross-country race and a teacher sees. What happens? | He is taken off the results | He wins a medal anyway for speed | Nothing, corners don't count | The teacher moves the corner
exercise: What is the wise choice the night before a spelling test you haven't learned? | Practise the words, even if the mark is low | Write them on your hand in small letters | Plan to peek at a friend's | Pretend to be ill
test: «copied Josh's maths 3 times, never got caught, easy». What is the truth? | It can still be noticed any day, and Josh can get in trouble too | It's only cheating in tests, not in homework, so it doesn't count | It's fine after three times | It's fine if Josh agrees
test: Teacher: «Interesting. You and Josh made the exact same mistake in question 4.» What should Max say? | It was me who copied, not Josh's fault | Josh copied me, miss, not the other way | It's a coincidence, miss | The question was wrong
test: Mum's text: «Mrs Kelly wants to see us after school about your project. What's going on?» What's the honest reply? | «I didn't make it, you did. Sorry» | «No idea, she's weird» | «Someone broke my tower» | «She's mixing me up with someone»
test: Max, furious: «you took my console?! it's NOT FAIR!» What does Mum mean when she says «It's a consequence, not revenge»? | It is to help him learn, not to hurt him back | She is angry and wants him to suffer | She will never give it back | She is joking
test: Ella's mate: «just peek at Mia's sheet, she's always right». What is the wise answer? | No, I'd rather get a low mark that's mine | Okay, but only for the hard words I don't know | Okay, if Mia doesn't see | Okay, peeking isn't copying
test: Coach, sharp: «You went down before he touched you. Yellow card.» What would put it right? | Say sorry to the other team's player | Argue that he did touch him | Do it again, but better | Blame the grass
test: Kid: «got 10/10 by peeking and nobody knows, so who cares». What is true? | The mark is empty, and he still doesn't know the words | Nobody knows, so it counts as a real mark that he earned | Peeking counts as learning | It only matters in big tests
test: Next morning Max is still sulking. What helps most? | Saying sorry and making his own project | Not speaking to Mum for a week | Asking Dad to give the console back secretly | Breaking the project
test: «answers r in the back lol just copy em». Why is that bad advice? | You learn nothing, and the teacher sees there's no working | The answers in the back are wrong | Teachers don't mind | It takes longer than doing the sums
test: Which is TRUE about cheating that is never found out? | It still leaves you not knowing, and often feeling guilty | It is completely harmless | It turns into real learning later | It is only wrong if found out
test: A boy cut the corner in the race. Next year what is the best way to put it right? | Run the whole course honestly | Cut a smaller corner | Not enter at all | Run only when no teacher is there
test: Friend whispers: «ill text u the answers under the desk». What should you do? | Say no and do your own test | Say yes, but only for one answer | Ask for the answers by note instead | Tell him to whisper it instead
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning repeated copying which has hitherto escaped detection. | Prior escape from detection affords no assurance against discovery, which commonly arises from identical errors | Repeated success renders copying permissible | Copying is permissible in respect of homework but not of tests | Undetected copying involves no consequence to any person
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a mark obtained by copying is of no value to the pupil. | It does not reflect the knowledge of the pupil | It is invariably lower than an honest mark | It is not recorded by the school | It is of value provided it remains undiscovered
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the withdrawal of a games console by parents following misconduct at school? | It is a consequence intended to instruct and not an act of revenge | It is an act of revenge on the part of the parents | It is an arbitrary measure without purpose | It relieves the child of any duty to make amends
exam: Determine the most appropriate conduct of a pupil who has submitted as his own a project constructed by a parent. | He ought to admit the matter and undertake a project of his own | He ought to maintain that he constructed it | He ought to withdraw the project without explanation | He ought to attribute the error to the teacher
